A||||6||86SE||Temp guage not working + bad gas mileage = relationship?||||5||
Z||000000||86SE||05-17-2001||12:06 AM||||About a month ago I was driving my SE to school when the temp bounced way up to almost in the red.  So I slowed down to the speed limit and fliped on the a/c to trigger the fan just incase it wasent on already.  Got to school fine, and made it home fine.  But on the way home my temp needle barely moved! I moved just one notch above the 100 degree mark.  Hmm, checked the coolant level, full.<P>And now I get about 10 miles per gallon, not good the way prices are looking.  If my temp sensor went out, like I think it did, how much would it affect my mileage, if any??<P>My car needs a tune up too.  Hasent had one for about 3.5 years/15,000 miles.  I plan on buying autolite wires and autolite platinum plugs for about $60.  That should help mileage some.<P>I would like to get at least 20-30 MGP if at all possible.  Last summer on the way down to Omaha to get my Fiero painted I averaged about 30-35 MPG on the interstate.  This 10-15 MPG stuff really sucks!!<P>HELP!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://fierodude.hypermart.net/mysig.jpg"> <BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero -- 3.4L Conversion *SUSPENDED* --<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||5
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||05-17-2001||12:18 AM||||the sender for the temp gauge and the sensor the ECM uses to determine engine temp are two separate things, they have nothing to do with each other.<P>  If your engine really did overheat its possible the ECM logged some error codes and is now locked into limp-home-mode which would cause your lousy mileage.<P>  Or its possible your temp gauge just happened to flake out about the same time your engine started to really need that overdue tuneup.<P>  You need to get your head under that hood and see whats going on back there. Check the ECM for error codes and then clear them and see if they come back.<P>  And next time your temp gauge or oil pressure gauge goes into the red zone, please stop and at least try to determin if the engine really is overheating, or low on oil, or leaking antifreeze... A few minutes of delay on the way to school can save you weeks of rebuilding-your-engine time.<P>  And if something is wrong with the car, dont drive it that way (overheating or out of oil, or whatever). let the thing cool down, or give it what it needs to survive the rest of the trip.||||reg||1
Z||000002||86SE||05-17-2001||12:22 AM||||As far as I know it never did actually overheat.  The second it got close to red I had to stop for a stop sign on the highway, so I got out and checked it out, engine was running fine, no coolant was boiling out and the check engine light never came on.<P>I figured it was the temp sensor right away because the guare dropped down to cold and only operated between the 100-120 degree mark.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://fierodude.hypermart.net/mysig.jpg"> <BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero -- 3.4L Conversion *SUSPENDED* --<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||1
Z||000003||KissMySSFiero||05-17-2001||12:31 AM||||sounds similar to what happened to me.  <BR>my temp guage pegged.  then all of a sudden it dropped down below normal operating temp.  i let it go a few days.  then decided i should work on it.  well looked at the t-stat to see if it was stuck open or something.  nope.  the little bar thats on the t-stat had bent.  seems to me that is got stuck closed.  the car got hot and built enough pressure to force the t-stat to press up and bend the arms to let water in.  needless to say i put another one in and havent had any problems since.   <BR>hope this helps.<P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>     <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>      <P>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">          <BR>"When in Doubt..Whip it out!!"||||reg||1
Z||000004||86SE||05-17-2001||12:39 AM||||Thanks, I'll try that.  Just checked my engine codes, all clear just flashed the code 12.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://fierodude.hypermart.net/mysig.jpg"> <BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero -- 3.4L Conversion *SUSPENDED* --<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||1
Z||000005||86SE||05-17-2001||01:33 AM||||Temporarily removed the thermostat to se if it makes the guage work.  Took a look at my air filter and to my surprise that K&N was PACKED with greasy dirt.  It was amazing how air still flowed through there!!<P>I just put it in last July and only put 5000 miles on it since then.  Dident even think about checking it yet, but glad I did.  We will see tomorrow if performance/gas milage and temp guage improves.  But for now I gotta go to bed.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Taijiguy||05-17-2001||08:01 AM||||Put the thermostat and a candy thermometer in a pan of water and put it on the stove. Monitor the thermostat as the water heats up, and use the thermometer to determine when the thermostat fully opens.||||reg||1
